// Where the signing team comes from — and the reason it is not in the
// project file.
//
// A Team ID is not a secret (it sits in every distributed app's embedded
// provisioning profile, readable with `codesign -dvv`). It is kept out of
// the repository so that a fork does not inherit someone else's team and
// meet signing errors it has no way to fix. Xcode writes DEVELOPMENT_TEAM
// straight into project.pbxproj the moment a team is picked in Signing &
// Capabilities, so leaving the setting only here — with nothing to override
// it in the target's own build settings — is what keeps it out. CI greps for
// it, because a build setting cannot enforce its own absence.
//
// To sign locally, create Support/Local.xcconfig (gitignored):
//
//     DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = ABCDE12345
//
// Without it, Debug builds still work — Release does not, and is not meant
// to: Debug ad-hoc-signs on macOS (see the target settings) precisely so the
// everyday loop needs no team, while Release is the distribution
// configuration and asks for a real one.
//
// Xcode Cloud does its own cloud signing and may resolve the team from the
// App Store Connect app record. If an archive there ever fails asking for a
// development team, write this same file from ci_scripts/ci_post_clone.sh
// using a workflow environment variable — that is the whole fix.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

#include? "Local.xcconfig"
